VARTOOLS — Linux Installation Notes
====================================

This file covers Linux-specific installation details.  The main ``README``
covers platform-independent material (dependencies, general ``configure``
options, ``pyvartools``, etc.); read it first.

Linux is the primary development platform for VARTOOLS.  Most distributions
can build the C binary and all optional dependencies from packaged software.


1. Prerequisites
----------------

Required:

* A C compiler (``gcc`` or ``clang``).
* A Fortran compiler (``gfortran``) — required for some command extensions.
* ``make``.
* ``libtool`` (for the dynamic-library support that VARTOOLS uses to load
  user-compiled commands and functions).

Optional (each enables additional features — see the main README for a full
table of what depends on what):

* ``libcfitsio-dev`` — FITS light curve support.
* ``libgsl-dev`` — ``-addnoise``, ``-microlens``, ``-resample``, ``-FFT``,
  ``-IFFT``, and the ``-FTP`` polynomial fast path.
* ``libnfft3-dev`` (pulls in ``libfftw3-dev``) — NFFT-batched summations
  in the ``-FTP`` command (~4x speedup at N >= 10000).  ``-FTP`` works
  without it, falling back to per-frequency direct sums.
* ``python3-dev`` + ``python3-numpy`` — embedded ``-python`` command.
* ``r-base-dev`` — embedded ``-R`` command.  Note that ``r-base`` alone is
  **not** sufficient; the ``-dev`` package provides ``libR.so`` and the R
  headers that ``configure`` needs to detect.

Only needed if you run ``./autogen.sh`` to regenerate the build system
(normally the pre-generated ``configure`` script in the distribution tarball
is sufficient):

* ``autoconf``, ``automake``, ``libtool``.

1.1 Debian / Ubuntu
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Install everything required plus all optional dependencies::

    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install -y \
        build-essential gfortran libtool \
        libcfitsio-dev libgsl-dev libnfft3-dev \
        python3-dev python3-numpy \
        r-base-dev

For ``./autogen.sh`` support, additionally::

    sudo apt-get install -y autoconf automake

1.2 Fedora / RHEL / Rocky / AlmaLinux
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

::

    sudo dnf install -y \
        gcc gcc-gfortran make libtool \
        cfitsio-devel gsl-devel \
        python3-devel python3-numpy \
        R-core-devel

NFFT3 is available on Fedora as ``nfft-devel`` (pulls in ``fftw-devel``)
but is not in the default RHEL / Rocky / AlmaLinux repositories.  On
Fedora, append ``nfft-devel`` to the install line; on RHEL-family distros
either build NFFT3 from source
(https://www-user.tu-chemnitz.de/~potts/nfft/) or skip it — ``-FTP``
works without NFFT3 using its direct-sum fallback.

For ``./autogen.sh`` support, additionally::

    sudo dnf install -y autoconf automake

1.3 Arch Linux / Manjaro
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

::

    sudo pacman -S --needed \
        base-devel gcc-fortran libtool \
        cfitsio gsl \
        python python-numpy \
        r

The NFFT3 library (used by ``-FTP`` for batched summations) is in the
AUR as ``nfft``; install with an AUR helper (e.g. ``yay -S nfft``) or
build from source.  ``-FTP`` falls back to per-frequency direct sums
without NFFT3.

1.4 CSPICE (all Linux distributions)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

CSPICE (used by ``-converttime`` for BJD/UTC conversions) is not in any
distro's package repository.  Download and build it from source::

    mkdir -p ~/src && cd ~/src
    wget https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/toolkit//C/PC_Linux_GCC_64bit/packages/cspice.tar.Z
    gunzip cspice.tar.Z
    tar -xf cspice.tar
    cd cspice
    csh makeall.csh       # (or "sh makeall.csh" on distros without csh)

This produces ``~/src/cspice/lib/cspice.a`` and the headers in
``~/src/cspice/include/``.  Pass these to ``configure`` with
``--with-cspice=/home/you/src/cspice`` (see below).

You will also want the generic NAIF kernel files for actual use — see the
``CSPICE`` section in the main README.


2. Build and install
--------------------

Unpack the tarball, then from the source directory::

    ./configure --prefix=$HOME/.local
    make
    make install

``configure`` auto-detects any optional library installed to a standard
location.  If a dependency lives somewhere non-standard, point ``configure``
at it::

    ./configure \
        --prefix=$HOME/.local \
        --with-cspice=$HOME/src/cspice \
        FC=gfortran F77=gfortran

See the main ``README`` for the full list of ``--with-*`` flags and for
typical ``CPPFLAGS`` / ``LDFLAGS`` overrides when libraries are in unusual
places.

After ``make install``, verify with::

    $HOME/.local/bin/vartools -help

If ``$HOME/.local/bin`` is on your ``PATH`` (typical), plain ``vartools``
works directly.


3. pyvartools (Python wrapper)
------------------------------

After the C binary is installed, install the Python wrapper::

    cd python
    pip install -e .

The wrapper finds the ``vartools`` binary on ``PATH`` automatically, and
uses the shared library ``libvartoolspipeline.so`` (installed by
``make install`` alongside the binary) for in-process execution.  See the
main ``README`` and the website's *Python API* section for details.


4. Typical problems
-------------------

* **``configure: error: Fortran 77 compiler cannot create executables``** —
  install ``gfortran``.  The default ``F77`` / ``FC`` on many systems
  points at a stub that fails.  Pass ``FC=gfortran F77=gfortran`` on the
  ``configure`` line.

* **``error while loading shared libraries: libcfitsio.so.*``** at runtime —
  your shell can't find the CFITSIO runtime library.  If you installed
  CFITSIO from source to a non-standard prefix, add its ``lib`` directory
  to ``LD_LIBRARY_PATH``, or use ``--with-cfitsio`` during ``configure`` so
  the built binary has the correct RPATH.

* **``configure`` silently skips R** — you have ``r-base`` but not
  ``r-base-dev``.  Install ``r-base-dev`` (Debian/Ubuntu) or
  ``R-core-devel`` (Fedora) and re-run ``configure``.

* **``configure`` can't find the numpy header** — make sure ``python3`` on
  your ``PATH`` is the same interpreter that has ``numpy`` installed.  The
  configure script runs ``python3 -c "import numpy; print(numpy.get_include())"``;
  if this fails, the embedded ``-python`` command is disabled.
